9. Sensitive Data Detection

Microsoft Presidio is the initial candidate for PII detection.

The PoC should support extension through custom recognisers.

Initial categories:

PERSON
EMAIL
PHONE
LOCATION
ADDRESS
CREDIT_CARD
BANK_ACCOUNT
API_KEY
PASSWORD
SECRET

Business-specific entities may include:

CUSTOMER
EMPLOYEE
SUPPLIER
CONTRACT_ID
ACCOUNT_ID
# 10. Tokenisation

Tokenisation must be reversible within the trusted environment.

For PoC v0.1, token mappings are scoped to a single request/session.

The PoC does NOT require token identity to remain stable across
different sessions or separate document uploads.

Example:

Request A:

    Tesla Australia -> CUSTOMER_001

Request B:

    Tesla Australia -> CUSTOMER_001

The token value MAY be different because the requests are independent.

The mapping MUST be destroyed when the request/session lifecycle ends,
subject to debugging requirements that do not expose sensitive data.

The implementation should provide an abstraction that allows a
persistent secure token vault to be introduced during the MVP phase.

The PoC must not assume that token identifiers are globally unique
across sessions.

Token mappings must remain entirely inside the trusted environment.

11. Data Minimisation

The gateway should avoid sending information that is not required for
the requested task.

Example:

If the user asks:

"Which customers have the highest gross margin?"

The LLM may only require:

customer token
revenue
cost
margin

It does not require:

customer address
phone number
bank account
employee information
12. LLM Provider Abstraction

The application should not directly depend on a single LLM provider.

Define a provider interface conceptually similar to:

LLMProvider
    |
    +-- OpenAIProvider
    +-- ClaudeProvider
    +-- GeminiProvider

The PoC may implement only one provider initially.

The architecture should allow additional providers later.

13. Response Protection

LLM output is considered untrusted.

Before returning a response to the user:

Scan for sensitive data.
Scan for unexpected token leakage.
Validate task compliance.
Apply output policy.
Restore tokens only within the trusted environment.
14. Logging

Logs must support troubleshooting and audit without exposing sensitive
information.

Never log:

raw PII
token mappings
API keys
credentials
full confidential prompts
full confidential responses

Logs may contain:

request ID
user ID or pseudonymous identifier
timestamp
provider
model
policy decision
processing status
security event type
15. Failure Behaviour

Security-sensitive failures should fail closed.

Examples:

If:

PII detection fails
tokenisation fails
policy evaluation fails
response validation fails

the gateway should NOT silently send raw data to the external LLM.

# 16. PoC Technology

## Runtime

- Python 3.12+

## Document Processing

- pandas
- openpyxl

## Sensitive Data Detection

Microsoft Presidio Analyzer will be used as the primary detection
framework.

Presidio does not by itself provide complete Chinese NLP capability.
Therefore the PoC must explicitly support a Chinese NLP backend.

The initial implementation should evaluate:

- spaCy Chinese model
- custom regex recognisers
- custom recognisers for business-specific entities

The exact Chinese NLP model must be validated against the synthetic
benchmark before being accepted.

## Chinese PII

The initial benchmark must include:

- Chinese person names
- Chinese addresses
- phone numbers
- email addresses
- synthetic Taiwan ID-like values
- synthetic company names

Format-based identifiers should primarily use deterministic
recognisers/regular expressions rather than relying solely on
statistical NER.

## Testing

- pytest
- precision
- recall
- F1

## Containerisation

- Docker

## LLM

One external LLM provider for the initial PoC.

The provider must be abstracted behind an LLMProvider interface.

## Important

Claude Code MUST NOT silently select an alternative NLP architecture
that changes the security or detection design.

If the selected Chinese NLP backend cannot achieve the defined
benchmark requirements, the implementation should report the issue
rather than weakening the acceptance criteria.

17. Future MVP

Potential MVP capabilities:

Web UI
SSO
RBAC
policy engine
multiple LLM providers
secure token vault
PostgreSQL
audit dashboard
document connectors
enterprise DLP integration
rate limiting
stronger prompt injection detection
18. Design Principles
Security boundary first.
Data minimisation.
Least privilege.
Fail closed.
Treat LLM output as untrusted.
Treat uploaded documents as untrusted.
Keep token mappings internal.
Prefer deterministic security controls over LLM-based security controls.
Keep PoC architecture simple.
Make security properties testable.

# 19. Open Questions

## OQ-001 — Chinese NLP Backend

The PoC requires Chinese PII detection.

The initial implementation should evaluate a Chinese spaCy model
combined with deterministic custom recognisers.

Final model selection is subject to benchmark results.

---

## OQ-002 — Token Lifetime

PoC token mappings are scoped to a single request/session.

Cross-session identity consistency is intentionally deferred to MVP.

---

## OQ-003 — Persistent Token Vault

The PoC may use an in-memory/local implementation.

MVP should evaluate:

- encrypted persistent storage
- Vault/KMS integration
- key rotation
- retention
- access control

---

## OQ-004 — LLM Provider

Only one provider is required for the PoC.

The architecture must remain provider-independent.

---

## OQ-005 — Prompt Injection Detection

The PoC will use deterministic/pattern-based detection as an initial
control.

More advanced ML/LLM-based detection may be evaluated during MVP.

---

## OQ-006 — Production Compliance

The PoC does not claim compliance with:

- ISO 27001
- SOC 2
- Australian Privacy Act
- GDPR
- industry-specific regulatory requirements

A dedicated compliance assessment is required before production use.
